        <li><strong>Australian super in pension phase is tax-free in Australia</strong> — withdrawals after 60 are not taxed by the ATO.</li>
        <li><strong>Spain does not recognise Australian super as tax-free.</strong> Under Spanish domestic law and the Australia-Spain treaty, pension/superannuation income received by a Spanish resident is generally taxable in Spain as the country of residence.</li>
        <li><strong>Spanish progressive tax rates on pension income:</strong> 19% (up to €12,450), rising to 47% on income over €300,000. For most retirees, the effective rate will be 19–30%.</li>
        <li><strong>The Australia-Spain treaty:</strong> Does not fully replicate the Australian tax-free super treatment. The treaty allocates pension taxation rights to the country of residence (Spain) in most cases.</li>
        <li><strong>ATO departing Australia implications:</strong> When you leave Australia to become a Spanish resident, you are generally treated as ceasing Australian tax residency. File a final Australian tax return. Notify your super fund and Services Australia.</li>
      </ul>
      <ul style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        <li><strong>Age Pension and non-residency:</strong> The Australian Age Pension can be paid overseas, but means-testing and eligibility rules apply for long-term non-residents. Review your entitlement before departing.</li>
        <li><strong>Spanish wealth tax (Patrimonio):</strong> Spain imposes an annual wealth tax on net assets above certain thresholds. Australian super fund balances and other assets held globally may be within scope. Regional rates vary significantly across Spain's regions.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

