        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions: Spain Retirement Visa for Australians</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="msv__faq-list">
        <details>
          <summary>What is the Spain retirement visa for Australians?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>The Spain retirement visa for Australians is the Non-Lucrative Visa (NLV). It allows Australian citizens to live in Spain long-term without working, provided they demonstrate sufficient passive income — approximately €2,400/month for a single applicant in 2026. Australian Age Pension, superannuation income streams, private pension, rental income, and investment income all count. Applications go through the Spanish Consulate in Sydney or Melbourne.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What is the AFP criminal record check and how do I get it?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Australian NLV applicants must provide an Australian Federal Police (AFP) National Police Check — not a state police check. Apply via the AFP website (afp.gov.au) or an accredited body. Processing is usually 1–2 weeks. The AFP check must then be apostilled through DFAT and translated into Spanish by a sworn translator.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does Australian superannuation count as income for the Spain retirement visa?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Superannuation income streams (regular withdrawals) can count toward the NLV income requirement. However, super has complex tax implications when you become a Spanish resident — income that is tax-free in Australia may be taxable in Spain. Always seek specialist cross-border tax advice from a professional with Australian-Spanish expertise before relying on super income for your NLV application or before making the move.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How does the Australia-Spain tax treaty work for Australian retirees?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Australia and Spain have a double taxation agreement, but it is considered less favourable for retirees than some other treaties. As a Spanish tax resident, Spain taxes your worldwide income including Australian pension and superannuation. Spanish income tax rates are progressive (19–47%). The treaty provides relief mechanisms but does not replicate Australian tax-free super treatment. Specialist advice is essential.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Where do I apply for the Spain retirement visa in Australia?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Apply in person at the Spanish Consulate General in Sydney (covering NSW, QLD, SA, NT, WA, ACT, TAS) or the Spanish Consulate in Melbourne (covering Victoria). Book appointments in advance through the consulate's online system. In-person attendance is required — applications cannot be submitted by post for the NLV.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

